1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,059
From lightsabers and X-wing fighters to bionic bad guys and high-tech gadgets of every shape, size and personality.

2
00:00:17,059 --> 00:00:18,980
The beast will be destroyed to show.

3
00:00:19,359 --> 00:00:25,359
From the mind of George Lucas came an epic saga about a galaxy far, far away.

4
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:26,699
I'm going to make the jump to light speed.

5
00:00:26,699 --> 00:00:36,000
But just how much of the amazing technology seen in the Star Wars saga is actually based on scientific fact?

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,379
Help me Obi-Wan Kenobi.

7
00:00:37,379 --> 00:00:40,519
And how much is simply a Jedi mind trick?

8
00:00:40,519 --> 00:00:44,659
There's a lot of elements in the movies that are coming to be true.

9
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,780
Could a lightsaber really work?

10
00:00:48,179 --> 00:00:56,280
And just how plausible is the science behind the world's best-known half-man, half-machine, Darth Vader?

11
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:00,540
Present technology is catching up with Star Wars and surpassing it.

12
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:05,200
So hop in your landspeeder and get ready to take an incredible journey.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,120
As we test the science fact and science fiction of Star Wars tech.

14
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:17,820
Here they come.

15
00:01:17,819 --> 00:01:31,599
When you say the word Star Wars, most people probably picture gigantic spaceships shooting across the galaxy in a burst of digital surround sound.

16
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:39,699
But while those huge explosions are certainly spectacular, how realistic are they?

17
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:42,659
In Star Wars, you hear a lot of sound.

18
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,460
But actually in space, there would be no sound.

19
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:49,180
Sound requires air.

20
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:53,480
The reason you can hear me is because my voice is actually vibrating the air.

21
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:54,960
Space is basically a vacuum.

22
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,520
Sound only travels through material interacting with the air around it.

23
00:01:58,859 --> 00:02:02,240
So if you watch the ship explode, you wouldn't hear anything.

24
00:02:02,239 --> 00:02:12,560
There is sound in space in Star Wars films just to add dramatic emphasis to the shots.

25
00:02:15,139 --> 00:02:18,579
In a visual effect shot, sometimes sound effects actually sell the shot.

26
00:02:21,799 --> 00:02:24,039
Without the sound effect, it just doesn't work.

27
00:02:24,039 --> 00:02:30,060
Big fiery explosions in space are another fantasy.

28
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:34,419
Such a big explosion needs more oxygen than you would get from the limited oxygen in a ship.

29
00:02:34,699 --> 00:02:37,560
So fires would be much smaller than you actually see in Star Wars.

30
00:02:38,579 --> 00:02:41,659
The ships themselves being hit would be presumably flammable.

31
00:02:43,739 --> 00:02:47,859
So the smoke that you get when they're hit, you would get whether you're in the atmosphere or not.

32
00:02:48,159 --> 00:02:49,060
So that's reasonable.

33
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:54,860
But not everything in a Star Wars space battle strays far from fact.

34
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:58,280
Take X-Wing fighters for example.

35
00:02:58,599 --> 00:03:01,259
The preferred craft of the heroic Rebel Alliance.

36
00:03:01,379 --> 00:03:03,560
R2, that stabilizer is broken loose again.

37
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:04,539
See if you can't lock it down.

38
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:11,099
With a few modifications, this sleek and speedy flyer would fit right in with the fighter planes found on our planet.

39
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:18,840
George Lucas looked at the X-Wing fighter as kind of extrapolation of modern aircraft, jet fighters and things like that.

40
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:27,659
And I think that relationship that it looks like a fighter plane that people can connect with that.

41
00:03:27,979 --> 00:03:30,740
So you look at it and automatically people get what that is.

42
00:03:32,699 --> 00:03:39,800
But the X-Wing would need a lot more engine power than our fighter planes to make hairpin turns like this in outer space.

43
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:46,320
In terms of maneuverability, you're out in space so you can't use the atmosphere to generate any of the forces on you.

44
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:51,980
An airplane turns and banks because it gets forces on the wings.

45
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:53,920
In space you don't have that to use.

46
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:57,700
So you need engines that can send out in all directions if you're going to turn a lot.

47
00:03:57,699 --> 00:04:05,139
You'd have to have a bunch of different thrusters mounted all over the vehicle and they'd have to operate in perfect unison in order to get those kinds of coordinated turns.

48
00:04:06,619 --> 00:04:09,859
Those kinds of turns and movements, it's not really possible.

49
00:04:09,860 --> 00:04:17,400
In battle, the X-Wings meet their match when confronted by the deadly Imperial TIE Fighters.

50
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:19,500
Hit but not bad.

51
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:20,699
R2, see what you can do with it.

52
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:25,560
But here's one Star Wars technology that's a lot closer to our galaxy than you might think.

53
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:29,960
The TIE in TIE Fighter stands for Twin Ion Engine.

54
00:04:30,379 --> 00:04:32,560
A type of engine that really does exist.

55
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,959
HYE check hydraulic pressure go.

56
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:39,240
Hydraulics go.

57
00:04:39,839 --> 00:04:46,079
In 1998, NASA launched a spacecraft known as Deep Space One on a data gathering mission.

58
00:04:47,199 --> 00:04:55,199
Main engine start and liftoff of the Delta rocket with Deep Space One, testing the spacecraft technologies of the next century.

59
00:04:55,199 --> 00:05:02,699
After launch, the spacecraft was propelled on its journey across the solar system by a single ion engine.

60
00:05:02,699 --> 00:05:06,839
The concept for ion engines has actually been around for more than 100 years.

61
00:05:07,219 --> 00:05:13,659
Now, the way it works is xenon gas, which is like helium or neon, but heavier, is introduced into a chamber here.

62
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,539
And it's ionized, which means it's given an electric charge.

63
00:05:17,019 --> 00:05:25,500
And once it has an electric charge, a voltage applied between this grid and one just behind it causes the xenon to shoot out of the engine.

64
00:05:25,839 --> 00:05:32,439
Well, the action of the xenon going out this direction causes a reaction that pushes the spacecraft in the other direction.

65
00:05:32,699 --> 00:05:36,060
And that's how the ion thruster propels the spacecraft.

66
00:05:37,219 --> 00:05:44,019
Ion engines are great for long space voyages that you don't mind getting there a little bit slowly.

67
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:51,399
The problem is, while the particles have very high velocity, which is good, they have very low mass.

68
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:56,740
And it takes a lot of energy to accelerate just a few particles, so we can only do a few at a time.

69
00:05:56,740 --> 00:06:04,160
In fact, the ion engines that we use push on a spacecraft about as hard as this single piece of paper pushes on my hand.

70
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:11,439
And so if you're being chased by an enemy who's trying to kill you, I think you'd probably opt for maneuverability over fuel efficiency.

71
00:06:11,439 --> 00:06:21,660
Not only does the speed of the Empire's TIE Fighters push the limits of current science, so do their large windshields.

72
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:28,579
Which can also be found on many other Star Wars spacecraft, like Han Solo's Millennium Falcon.

73
00:06:28,579 --> 00:06:34,319
Sir, the possibility of successfully navigating an asteroid field is approximately 3,720 to 1.

74
00:06:34,339 --> 00:06:35,420
Never tell me the odds.

75
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:41,360
It's very difficult to manufacture glass that can withstand the vacuum pressure exerted by space.

76
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:44,759
And also stand up to occasional encounters with floating debris.

77
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:54,160
That's why the windows on real spaceships tend to be very small and up to 2 and a half inches thick.

78
00:06:54,939 --> 00:06:57,480
But what if those windows weren't made of glass?

79
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:03,560
One possibility that's very exciting that's a recent development is a plasma window.

80
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:07,240
Plasma is the fourth state of matter.

81
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:10,399
Solid, liquid, gas, plasma.

82
00:07:10,399 --> 00:07:16,899
Plasma is like a gas that's been very electrified, so it has separated positive and negative charges.

83
00:07:16,899 --> 00:07:22,959
Like what's in a neon light, you have a tube to contain the plasma, which then glows and creates light.

84
00:07:22,959 --> 00:07:28,399
Plasma has different properties than a gas because you have these charged particles moving around.

85
00:07:28,399 --> 00:07:30,519
They create electric and magnetic fields.

86
00:07:30,519 --> 00:07:34,959
And you can contain the plasma with electric and magnetic fields.

87
00:07:34,959 --> 00:07:43,279
In 1995, physicist Adi Hershkovich did exactly that when he created the first plasma window.

88
00:07:44,079 --> 00:07:48,799
It was tiny compared to windows in Star Wars ships, only 2 inches in diameter.

89
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:53,980
But it opened up the possibility of having gigantic space windows in the future.

90
00:07:53,980 --> 00:08:02,300
Plasma, because of its density, can hold in atmosphere on one side and keep out space on the other side.

91
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:13,199
And so it's something that could potentially work as a kind of force wall constrained within the boundaries of, say, a window opening or, say, the Death Star landing bay,

92
00:08:13,199 --> 00:08:18,759
and virtually be a wall between the vacuum and the atmosphere.

93
00:08:20,759 --> 00:08:26,079
Another impressive feature of the spaceships in Star Wars is their ability to jump to light speed.

94
00:08:26,079 --> 00:08:27,959
Strap yourselves in. I'm going to make the jump to light speed.

95
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:35,080
Unfortunately, in our galaxy, spacecraft can't travel anywhere near the speed of light, much less beyond it.

96
00:08:35,519 --> 00:08:39,980
In our everyday experience, we think, okay, if I want to go twice as fast, I need twice as much energy.

97
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:44,200
The amount of energy you need to go a little bit faster, it's all proportional.

98
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:49,860
So going four times faster would take four times the amount of energy, and so on.

99
00:08:50,259 --> 00:08:50,920
Simple, right?

100
00:08:51,460 --> 00:08:52,820
Well, here's the problem.

101
00:08:53,159 --> 00:09:00,420
According to Einstein's special theory of relativity, as any object with mass or weight goes faster, it actually gets heavier.

102
00:09:01,139 --> 00:09:05,139
The amount of energy you need to go a little bit faster just keeps getting bigger and bigger and bigger,

103
00:09:05,300 --> 00:09:06,980
and it goes to infinity, essentially.

104
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:15,060
The more mass you have, the more energy it takes to accelerate to a given speed.

105
00:09:15,159 --> 00:09:16,019
Ready for light speed?

106
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:20,200
One, two, three!

107
00:09:20,379 --> 00:09:24,920
To accelerate something with mass to the speed of light would require pretty much infinite energy.

108
00:09:25,460 --> 00:09:27,220
Currently, no known way to do this.

109
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:28,060
It's not fair.

110
00:09:28,059 --> 00:09:39,939
The Millennium Falcon may have speed, but when it comes to sheer power, there's nothing that can beat the Death Star.

111
00:09:40,819 --> 00:09:49,739
In his original script for Episode 4, A New Hope, George Lucas envisioned a space station so huge it could carry fleets of TIE fighters,

112
00:09:49,740 --> 00:09:55,580
legions of stormtroopers, and hold enough energy to blow up an entire planet.

113
00:09:56,820 --> 00:10:03,620
An international group of space agencies has been working on the International Space Station for many, many years now.

114
00:10:03,779 --> 00:10:07,220
It's microscopic in comparison to the size of the Death Star.

115
00:10:07,220 --> 00:10:15,700
About the size of two football fields, the International Space Station might seem pretty big, but the Death Star's got it beat by miles.

116
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:21,700
At 160 kilometers in diameter, it's nearly 900 times larger.

117
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:28,720
And the Death Star's immense size doesn't keep it from orbiting planets or traveling across a star system.

118
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:35,280
It actually would have pretty modest propulsion needs since it's just in a normal Keplerian orbit, just like the Earth is around the Sun.

119
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:38,820
It really would only need small thrusts to change its trajectory.

120
00:10:39,740 --> 00:10:41,580
We're approaching the planet Yavin.

121
00:10:41,980 --> 00:10:44,200
The Rebel base is on the moon on the far side.

122
00:10:44,660 --> 00:10:46,420
We are preparing to orbit the planet.

123
00:10:46,419 --> 00:10:53,299
But to get the Death Star moving fast or to make it change direction requires a tremendous amount of power.

124
00:10:53,579 --> 00:10:56,240
And that's something that's probably very difficult to achieve.

125
00:10:56,959 --> 00:10:59,979
A more plausible feature of the Death Star is gravity.

126
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:05,219
Because it's so huge, the Death Star has a certain amount of natural gravity.

127
00:11:05,539 --> 00:11:07,579
There would be gravity on the surface of it.

128
00:11:07,699 --> 00:11:09,919
It would be detectable, but very, very weak.

129
00:11:10,339 --> 00:11:14,740
As you went toward the center, there would be less and less gravity.

130
00:11:14,740 --> 00:11:16,299
When you got to the middle, there would be zero.

131
00:11:16,759 --> 00:11:19,460
So they would have to provide some other gravity field.

132
00:11:19,539 --> 00:11:20,740
You'd have to generate that.

133
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:29,220
So what would it take to let Darth Vader and his stormtroopers stroll leisurely down corridors while plotting to take over the galaxy?

134
00:11:29,659 --> 00:11:34,000
One of the interesting things is that you can make a pseudo-gravity field by rotating the Death Star.

135
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:41,120
If it's spinning on a north-south axis, centrifugal forces would be generated and it would feel like gravity on the inner wall of the Death Star.

136
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:44,200
The problem is people would be walking on the surface of the walls.

137
00:11:44,399 --> 00:11:49,100
So you'd have to turn the hallway sideways to make it look like people were walking on the floor normally.

138
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:51,920
So you could walk around out there.

139
00:11:52,019 --> 00:11:53,139
People have done the calculations.

140
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:55,580
It's not that hard to make it spin that fast.

141
00:11:55,820 --> 00:12:01,639
But for all its amazing capabilities, there was one thing the Death Star couldn't overcome.

142
00:12:02,159 --> 00:12:03,500
A fatal design flaw.

143
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:09,440
One that allowed a lone pilot with a keen eye and the help of the Force let go.

144
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:13,520
To blow the whole thing to smithereens.

145
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:21,300
Light is coming in.

146
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:27,840
But although some of Star Wars' amazing technologies seem to be light years away from possibility here on Earth,

147
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:31,100
others are almost within our grasp.

148
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:34,240
If you only knew the power.

149
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:40,520
He's big.

150
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:43,640
He's bad.

151
00:12:44,280 --> 00:12:46,780
I find your lack of faith disturbing.

152
00:12:47,560 --> 00:12:49,400
And he's bionic.

153
00:12:50,600 --> 00:12:53,840
He's Darth Vader, the ultimate screen villain.

154
00:12:54,280 --> 00:12:55,440
You are big to me.

155
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:57,540
It is useless to resist.

156
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:01,840
He was born Anakin Skywalker.

157
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:05,420
A clean-cut, likable kid with a talent for the Force.

158
00:13:05,419 --> 00:13:09,659
Who grew up and lost his soul to the dark side.

159
00:13:09,719 --> 00:13:12,019
Anakin, become my apprentice.

160
00:13:12,679 --> 00:13:14,939
But that wasn't the only thing he lost.

161
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:18,199
First, his right arm was cut off in a duel.

162
00:13:21,839 --> 00:13:24,439
You underestimate my power.

163
00:13:25,779 --> 00:13:26,679
Don't try it.

164
00:13:26,679 --> 00:13:36,579
Then, after a battle with Obi-Wan Kenobi, he lost the rest of his limbs, suffered severe burns to his body, and damage to his lungs.

165
00:13:39,519 --> 00:13:47,659
Advanced prosthetics were able to keep him alive and turned him into the universe's most feared half-man, half-machine.

166
00:13:47,659 --> 00:13:55,579
But could anyone, even with the help of bionics, sustain such extensive injuries?

167
00:13:55,740 --> 00:13:56,959
Can you hear me?

168
00:13:57,299 --> 00:13:58,059
And survive?

169
00:14:00,620 --> 00:14:06,199
When that happens, when he's on the edge of the volcano, he could survive that, if he's in good shape.

170
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:14,320
The burning that happened could actually help cauterize the blood flow, so it could reduce the loss of blood.

171
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:17,040
The searing of the lungs, etc., could be lethal, of course.

172
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:20,140
It's just a matter of how much and how poisonous the gas and the amount of heat.

173
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:29,020
The thing I would be most worried about would be his lungs, because breathing in that super-hot air can burn severely.

174
00:14:29,019 --> 00:14:35,659
He seems to have continuous lung problems, and he probably had permanent damage to his lung tissue,

175
00:14:35,860 --> 00:14:42,460
in which case he would need some sort of purified, enriched oxygen to be pumped into his lungs.

176
00:14:44,579 --> 00:14:49,419
Vader's breath mask was one of the first features George Lucas conceived for his character.

177
00:14:49,879 --> 00:14:56,419
The concept soon evolved to include the trademark black bodysuit that holds the key to Vader's survival.

178
00:14:56,419 --> 00:15:01,919
Within the suit and the system he has, he has to have sensors that access blood chemistry,

179
00:15:02,139 --> 00:15:06,879
the amount of oxygen and carbon dioxide, how much sugar's in his blood, his temperature, heart rate,

180
00:15:06,939 --> 00:15:08,500
all those normal physiological things.

181
00:15:08,659 --> 00:15:11,860
Those are then brought in, and through the chips that are on his chest,

182
00:15:12,059 --> 00:15:15,079
he's got to then have an output to correct them in a certain way,

183
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:17,959
by raising temperature, lowering glucose, whatever it is.

184
00:15:19,039 --> 00:15:20,019
Most impressive.

185
00:15:20,759 --> 00:15:22,719
And what's even more impressive?

186
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:28,720
The suit's combination of life-sustaining prosthetics and bionics really isn't that far-fetched.

187
00:15:29,440 --> 00:15:32,779
Obi-Wan has taught you well.

188
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:36,540
Take that noisy air pump that allows Vader to breathe.

189
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:41,840
A much quieter alternative is on the way.

190
00:15:42,519 --> 00:15:47,540
To replace Darth's lung with a prosthetic lung, this sort of technology exists right now.

191
00:15:47,539 --> 00:15:53,399
We just take, like, a lab sponge, like this, and we put it in the chest cavity.

192
00:15:53,799 --> 00:15:56,240
You notice that this sponge has got little holes in it,

193
00:15:56,459 --> 00:16:00,519
and you can put fluids in here and other compounds, growth factors.

194
00:16:00,819 --> 00:16:03,719
You can even have stem cells that migrate in here,

195
00:16:03,799 --> 00:16:07,679
and you want to reconstruct the same sort of layer that would be on the surface of the lungs.

196
00:16:08,099 --> 00:16:09,459
And this is being done right now.

197
00:16:09,519 --> 00:16:11,219
It's being done with a lot of different polymers.

198
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:13,000
Even fat cells are being re-engineered.

199
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:21,040
In order to have a sense of touch and feel temperature,

200
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:25,120
Vader's prosthetics would have to contain heat and pressure sensors,

201
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:31,879
another seemingly sci-fi technology that's currently available here on Earth.

202
00:16:32,940 --> 00:16:35,240
We have some great prosthetic limbs now.

203
00:16:35,399 --> 00:16:36,960
Say you have an artificial limb.

204
00:16:37,300 --> 00:16:38,960
You grab a coffee cup.

205
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:42,900
We now have sensors in the artificial fingers

206
00:16:42,900 --> 00:16:47,940
that are attached to your nerves up in your residual limb.

207
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:52,080
And so if it's hot coffee, the heat would be transmitted,

208
00:16:52,259 --> 00:16:54,519
and you would feel in a little plate the heat.

209
00:16:54,700 --> 00:16:59,500
If you are holding it too tightly, the pressure will make your limb tingle.

210
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:10,720
We would be honored if you would join us.

211
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:15,079
And in case you were wondering why you never see Darth Vader snacking on Empire cuisine,

212
00:17:15,759 --> 00:17:20,220
the Dark Lord gets all the nutrients he needs through a feeding tube in his suit.

213
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:27,100
But unfortunately, Vader's purely liquid diet could lead to gastrointestinal complications.

214
00:17:27,100 --> 00:17:31,500
One of the main problems with liquid nutrients is not a lot of bulk, a lot of fiber,

215
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:38,480
and the internal milieu, the flora within the gut, gets very unhappy, and it leads to sickness.

216
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:44,860
But what Vader lacks in a healthy diet is more than compensated for by his enormous strength.

217
00:17:44,980 --> 00:17:47,180
What have you done with those plans?

218
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:53,640
And that strength gets an extra boost from a generator inside his armor

219
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:57,660
that stimulates his muscles with powerful electrical impulses.

220
00:17:59,140 --> 00:18:02,380
Right now, prosthetic limbs are powered with little batteries.

221
00:18:02,380 --> 00:18:06,340
We're getting up to the lithium battery, which is what's on your laptop.

222
00:18:06,940 --> 00:18:12,200
I think in the future, we're going to be powering the limbs with biological power

223
00:18:12,200 --> 00:18:14,100
through using the power of our bodies.

224
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:18,380
They actually have an interesting biologically powered battery right now.

225
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:20,680
It's about the size of a credit card.

226
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:26,700
And if you urinate on this battery, that charges it up and it runs for a while.

227
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:31,240
So one might think that perhaps Darth Vader, I don't know,

228
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:35,059
maybe charging up his batteries in ways we don't want to think about,

229
00:18:35,420 --> 00:18:36,599
but they're under the cape.

230
00:18:37,559 --> 00:18:41,680
But of all the bionics used to put Darth Vader together again,

231
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:47,220
the most impressive is the technology that went into the creation of his artificial hands.

232
00:18:48,319 --> 00:18:54,720
It's something that also benefits Luke Skywalker in Episode 5, The Empire Strikes Back.

233
00:18:58,539 --> 00:19:00,740
Don't make me destroy you.

234
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:04,079
The hand is one of the body's most intricate structures.

235
00:19:04,819 --> 00:19:10,059
And Star Wars engineers have replicated its delicate dexterity down to the fingertips.

236
00:19:10,599 --> 00:19:14,279
And that technology is actually within our reach.

237
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:17,980
Present prosthetic devices have two or three degrees of freedom.

238
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:19,620
They're just movements like this.

239
00:19:19,700 --> 00:19:20,359
It's how many movements.

240
00:19:20,759 --> 00:19:24,420
The ones that you've seen this year have about four or five degrees of freedom.

241
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:27,620
What we need is 25 to do this, this, this, this, this, this.

242
00:19:27,619 --> 00:19:30,879
So that will be coming out in about a year, year and a half.

243
00:19:31,359 --> 00:19:34,119
So I would say that what we see in Star Wars is very plausible.

244
00:19:34,959 --> 00:19:40,859
On Earth, some hand prosthetics are controlled by contracting and relaxing residual muscles in the body.

245
00:19:40,859 --> 00:19:46,659
The movement that makes it work is just in here.

246
00:19:46,659 --> 00:19:49,079
There's a little micro switch.

247
00:19:50,079 --> 00:19:53,000
And the slightest touch makes it open.

248
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,119
And the slightest touch on this side.

249
00:19:56,119 --> 00:20:02,979
Basically, what I'm doing inside that cap is no more than that.

250
00:20:03,979 --> 00:20:08,519
But to duplicate Star Wars technology, there's still one missing piece of the puzzle.

251
00:20:09,359 --> 00:20:13,739
Just how can the human brain communicate with something like an artificial hand?

252
00:20:14,119 --> 00:20:17,679
The brain sends a signal to the residual muscles telling the hand to open.

253
00:20:17,680 --> 00:20:22,320
The computer senses that information from the residual muscle and the hand opens.

254
00:20:22,940 --> 00:20:25,759
Conversely, they can fire the muscles that would have caused their hand to close.

255
00:20:26,060 --> 00:20:27,100
And the hand can close.

256
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:31,620
With some of the more advanced control techniques, we're actually able to alter the types of signals

257
00:20:31,620 --> 00:20:34,519
that can go to the microprocessor and change functions.

258
00:20:34,700 --> 00:20:40,340
Make the hand do a pinch grip or if you're holding a Coke bottle or something along those lines.

259
00:20:40,339 --> 00:20:49,439
Fortunately, Luke Skywalker and even Darth Vader fared better at the hands of Star Wars surgeons than this fellow.

260
00:20:52,639 --> 00:20:55,199
What's the situation, Captain?

261
00:20:55,679 --> 00:21:01,199
A forerunner of Darth Vader, evil General Grievous became more machine than man.

262
00:21:01,439 --> 00:21:07,740
After doctors were only able to save his brain, eyes, internal organs, and spinal cord.

263
00:21:07,740 --> 00:21:10,299
Yes, Lord Sidious.

264
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:12,359
Grievous lost his arms and legs.

265
00:21:12,440 --> 00:21:15,519
At that point, his brain was still intact and was used to moving arms and legs.

266
00:21:15,799 --> 00:21:19,220
The question is whether you can take the signals that are already in his brain,

267
00:21:19,559 --> 00:21:22,240
take that intention and will to move, and turn it into action.

268
00:21:22,920 --> 00:21:27,579
We know the parts of the brain and the circuitries involved in some complex tasks.

269
00:21:27,900 --> 00:21:32,039
But the command signal, the output of all that brain electricity,

270
00:21:32,640 --> 00:21:35,960
what that really is, that code, we don't know that code yet, really.

271
00:21:35,960 --> 00:21:40,860
Bionic technology is a continually evolving science.

272
00:21:41,799 --> 00:21:46,799
Each year brings us closer to the day when the interchanging of human parts will be commonplace.

273
00:21:47,620 --> 00:21:53,019
That constant evolution consciously influenced George Lucas and his effects team

274
00:21:53,019 --> 00:21:55,660
when designing the bionics of General Grievous.

275
00:21:56,019 --> 00:21:59,440
Grievous was the alpha technology, and it didn't quite work.

276
00:21:59,700 --> 00:22:02,779
And so we tried to make him sort of heavy and clunky.

277
00:22:02,980 --> 00:22:04,880
We had him coughing and rasping.

278
00:22:04,880 --> 00:22:09,500
Because we were thinking the technology wasn't quite working,

279
00:22:09,620 --> 00:22:11,000
his lungs were filling with liquid.

280
00:22:12,860 --> 00:22:16,100
But he was still stronger because of the technology.

281
00:22:18,020 --> 00:22:21,060
But it wasn't until the beta version, that's Vader,

282
00:22:21,460 --> 00:22:27,000
that he became stronger and was not restricted as much with the technology as Grievous was.

283
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
Now I am the master.

284
00:22:29,700 --> 00:22:31,420
Only a master of evil, Darth.

285
00:22:34,420 --> 00:22:38,039
But not even Vader was endowed with this bionic bonus.

286
00:22:38,599 --> 00:22:40,079
An extra pair of arms.

287
00:22:40,740 --> 00:22:41,500
Attack!

288
00:22:41,980 --> 00:22:42,480
Kenobi!

289
00:22:42,480 --> 00:22:47,140
And those added limbs gave Grievous a unique advantage over his Jedi opponents.

290
00:22:47,140 --> 00:22:53,380
An ability to wield four of the most elegant, but deadly of all Star Wars technologies.

291
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:56,240
The lightsaber.

292
00:22:56,240 --> 00:23:00,940
What is it?

293
00:23:00,940 --> 00:23:03,620
It's your father's lightsaber.

294
00:23:04,500 --> 00:23:06,440
This is the weapon of a Jedi Knight.

295
00:23:07,180 --> 00:23:09,680
Not as clumsy or random as a blaster.

296
00:23:10,859 --> 00:23:15,940
An elegant weapon, but a more civilized age.

297
00:23:15,940 --> 00:23:21,360
The lightsaber has been a key part of the Star Wars arsenal since the saga began.

298
00:23:22,039 --> 00:23:28,160
George Lucas envisioned it simply as a handle that shoots out a laser beam about four feet long.

299
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:38,539
It's relatively lightweight and can cut through almost anything and everyone with one graceful stroke.

300
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:43,080
The weapon of choice for both Jedi and Sith.

301
00:23:43,460 --> 00:23:45,140
You're under arrest, Chancellor.

302
00:23:45,140 --> 00:23:49,360
But could a lightsaber actually exist?

303
00:23:50,360 --> 00:23:54,640
Could a beam of laser light really cut through everything from flesh and bone?

304
00:23:56,720 --> 00:23:58,600
To giant steel doors?

305
00:23:58,860 --> 00:23:59,820
This is impossible!

306
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:03,780
And is a lightsaber blade even made of light?

307
00:24:04,280 --> 00:24:08,900
Light is too insubstantial to accomplish what a lightsaber accomplishes.

308
00:24:09,520 --> 00:24:14,220
That is, you can't use light to repel an object, and yet lightsabers do that.

309
00:24:14,220 --> 00:24:17,440
So how does a lightsaber's laser beam work?

310
00:24:17,440 --> 00:24:19,440
And what exactly is a laser?

311
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:26,000
Laser stands for light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation, which is a complicated

312
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:32,400
way of saying that they're atomic processes that cause light to be emitted in a way that

313
00:24:32,400 --> 00:24:33,660
all of the light waves move together.

314
00:24:33,660 --> 00:24:39,880
In a laser, there's an interaction between the atoms and the light.

315
00:24:39,880 --> 00:24:45,060
Rather than this light being completely random, going off in all directions and having all colors,

316
00:24:45,060 --> 00:24:51,880
photons in a laser can group up, and that will give you this beam which will have these special properties.

317
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:58,760
But in spite of their visual similarity, a laser beam can't come close to doing the kind of tricks a lightsaber can.

318
00:25:02,100 --> 00:25:03,920
They crash into each other and clang.

319
00:25:04,920 --> 00:25:06,100
Of course, light doesn't do that.

320
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:10,680
At the end of the lightsaber, the light stops somehow.

321
00:25:11,820 --> 00:25:15,220
While in a laser, this beam will go on, practically speaking, forever.

322
00:25:15,220 --> 00:25:21,059
A beam of light will continue unless it is scattered, absorbed, or reflected.

323
00:25:21,720 --> 00:25:23,980
There's no way a lightsaber would be a laser.

324
00:25:25,720 --> 00:25:30,400
So when you think about it, if Yoda and Count Dooku swung two laser beams at each other,

325
00:25:30,819 --> 00:25:33,660
the result wouldn't exactly light up the box office.

326
00:25:34,039 --> 00:25:37,160
If you have two laser beams crossing like lightsabers do in a battle,

327
00:25:37,319 --> 00:25:39,480
it's really no different than two flashlights crossing,

328
00:25:39,880 --> 00:25:42,920
and the light beams just pass right through each other and have no influence.

329
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:49,400
Okay, so lasers are out.

330
00:25:49,759 --> 00:25:51,539
But how else could lightsabers work?

331
00:25:52,060 --> 00:25:54,680
Some people theorize that lightsabers use plasma.

332
00:25:54,900 --> 00:25:59,920
So when you think about plasma, you think about a glowing, gaseous-like substance.

333
00:26:00,860 --> 00:26:02,380
Stars are made out of plasma.

334
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:08,279
The sun, lightning bolts, fluorescent lights, they're all plasma.

335
00:26:08,840 --> 00:26:12,160
As we now know from our examination of the plasma window,

336
00:26:12,160 --> 00:26:17,340
this fourth state of matter is made of positively and negatively charged particles

337
00:26:17,340 --> 00:26:21,320
and can be constrained by both electric and magnetic fields.

338
00:26:21,640 --> 00:26:24,700
On Earth now, we can make a cylinder of plasma.

339
00:26:25,380 --> 00:26:30,460
So you can imagine a tall, long tube of plasma, just like the lightsaber.

340
00:26:30,460 --> 00:26:38,720
Two plasma lightsabers would not cut through each other because the magnetic fields would repel each other.

341
00:26:39,720 --> 00:26:46,319
And plasma, when heated to a high degree, will glow just like the lightsabers do.

342
00:26:46,680 --> 00:26:52,120
But in order to get plasma hot enough to slice through metal and people the way lightsabers do,

343
00:26:52,119 --> 00:26:56,899
it would have to be about ten times hotter than anything found here on Earth.

344
00:26:56,979 --> 00:26:57,500
Good!

345
00:26:57,719 --> 00:26:58,799
Anakin, good!

346
00:26:59,299 --> 00:27:01,859
About 200 million degrees, actually.

347
00:27:04,119 --> 00:27:09,119
Might this be better to mail it to your opponent, turn it on via remote control,

348
00:27:09,299 --> 00:27:11,219
and let him get a nice burn that way?

349
00:27:11,299 --> 00:27:15,439
I see you have constructed a new lightsaber.

350
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:20,440
Another problem is the lightsaber handle is only the size of a flashlight.

351
00:27:21,660 --> 00:27:25,019
Right now, we could generate something that looks like a lightsaber,

352
00:27:25,120 --> 00:27:28,519
but it would take a building full of equipment to do it.

353
00:27:28,880 --> 00:27:29,799
So that's a problem.

354
00:27:30,080 --> 00:27:34,600
But if we can solve those problems, which I think, you know, in the future everything gets smaller,

355
00:27:34,779 --> 00:27:38,100
we'll be able to make something like a lightsaber.

356
00:27:38,099 --> 00:27:45,599
But what about all those other high-tech weapons used by Star Wars heroes and villains?

357
00:27:46,559 --> 00:27:50,199
Loki religions and ancient weapons are no match for a good blaster at your side.

358
00:27:51,279 --> 00:27:58,000
Star Wars lore tells us that blaster guns operate by firing concentrated beams of high-energy particles.

359
00:27:59,319 --> 00:28:02,000
Plasma has highly charged particles, too.

360
00:28:02,480 --> 00:28:05,319
So could blaster technology also involve plasma?

361
00:28:05,319 --> 00:28:12,099
Blaster beams look like they might be made of plasma that's somehow been encapsulated or contained in a way to form a plasma bullet.

362
00:28:13,319 --> 00:28:14,899
This would be very deadly.

363
00:28:15,720 --> 00:28:20,500
You're sending something with a lot of energy through something and causing damage from the energy.

364
00:28:21,439 --> 00:28:28,799
But finding a way to encapsulate fast-moving plasma to form a blaster beam has Earth scientists stumped.

365
00:28:28,799 --> 00:28:35,839
Blaster guns, they shoot some piece of matter at an incredible speed.

366
00:28:37,059 --> 00:28:44,200
But if you have a plasma in free air, it will cool down and relax its heat with the surrounding air very quickly.

367
00:28:45,039 --> 00:28:47,460
I could not see how that could work in an atmosphere.

368
00:28:47,460 --> 00:28:55,819
But blasters aren't the only weapons that can project concentrated blasts of energy in the Star Wars galaxy.

369
00:28:56,920 --> 00:29:00,059
Let's say you find yourself under attack by big guns.

370
00:29:01,180 --> 00:29:03,039
Starting up the shield!

371
00:29:03,500 --> 00:29:06,600
The handiest tool to have would be a deflector sheath.

372
00:29:07,460 --> 00:29:08,680
And the bigger, the better.

373
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:15,039
Believe it or not, Earth scientists are developing just such a defensive technology.

374
00:29:15,580 --> 00:29:16,299
Open fire!

375
00:29:17,080 --> 00:29:23,060
But it'll be a while before they can make a shield tough enough to withstand a blast at point-blank range.

376
00:29:24,720 --> 00:29:27,759
Plasma could potentially be used as a shield.

377
00:29:28,180 --> 00:29:32,180
There's scientists now who's developing cold plasmas.

378
00:29:32,180 --> 00:29:38,200
Because if you had a hot plasma and you put it around the outside of your ship as a shield, it would tend to melt your ship.

379
00:29:38,740 --> 00:29:42,759
But if it was a cold plasma, it could potentially protect your ship.

380
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:44,920
It could deflect microwaves.

381
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:47,940
It could potentially deflect laser beams.

382
00:29:48,500 --> 00:29:52,799
They're doing this now to potentially shield satellites in orbit.

383
00:29:53,220 --> 00:29:58,860
In the world of Star Wars technology, it's possible for entire planets to have defense shields.

384
00:29:58,859 --> 00:30:05,079
But even they're no match for the deadliest of all Star Wars weapons, the Death Star super laser.

385
00:30:06,219 --> 00:30:11,019
The way the laser works can be compared to a magnifying glass when held under the sun.

386
00:30:11,659 --> 00:30:16,799
Both devices collect energy and intensify it into a single, searing beam of light.

387
00:30:18,299 --> 00:30:22,899
When the Death Star's light beams come together to make the super laser,

388
00:30:23,399 --> 00:30:26,599
in reality, those laser beams would just pass right through each other.

389
00:30:27,019 --> 00:30:27,859
They wouldn't combine.

390
00:30:27,859 --> 00:30:31,419
You can focus a single beam of light with a magnifying glass,

391
00:30:31,539 --> 00:30:35,159
but in the way the Death Star does it, it happens in empty space.

392
00:30:35,599 --> 00:30:38,740
That is, lasers come together and then go a different direction.

393
00:30:39,179 --> 00:30:43,899
And without some object there to redirect the laser beams, there's no way that could happen.

394
00:30:44,279 --> 00:30:46,359
We didn't actually have a lens out there.

395
00:30:46,539 --> 00:30:49,839
George talked about how there would be a condenser of some kind or other,

396
00:30:49,839 --> 00:30:54,159
even though it would be invisible, that would bring multiple laser bolts together

397
00:30:54,159 --> 00:30:56,939
and be able to shoot at an object.

398
00:30:56,940 --> 00:31:01,860
I have chosen to test this station's destructive power on your home planet of Alderaan.

399
00:31:01,859 --> 00:31:02,359
No.

400
00:31:02,359 --> 00:31:03,419
Commence primary ignition.

401
00:31:03,420 --> 00:31:10,720
So, once again, the Empire's R&D department seems to be light years ahead of anything we have on Earth,

402
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:18,600
especially since our scientists have yet to invent a weapon powerful enough to blow away a planet with a single blast.

403
00:31:23,160 --> 00:31:26,059
But not all Star Wars technology is so deadly.

404
00:31:26,059 --> 00:31:27,059
Sometimes, it can be a good time.

405
00:31:27,059 --> 00:31:28,819
Sometimes, it can be downright fun.

406
00:31:28,819 --> 00:31:36,339
Especially if you have to travel across deserts or through forests and cities at lightning speeds.

407
00:31:36,339 --> 00:31:47,619
From Tatooine to Endor.

408
00:31:47,619 --> 00:31:49,939
Move closer!

409
00:31:49,940 --> 00:31:51,940
And from Coruscant.

410
00:31:51,940 --> 00:31:52,940
To Naboo.

411
00:31:52,940 --> 00:31:53,940
To Naboo.

412
00:31:53,940 --> 00:32:05,860
The need for speed seems just as essential to the life forms of Star Wars as it is for thrill-seeking motorcyclists and race car drivers here on Earth.

413
00:32:05,859 --> 00:32:18,099
Not only are the planetary vehicles of Star Wars incredible to look at, they also come fully equipped with more technological innovations than you can shake a lightsaber at.

414
00:32:18,099 --> 00:32:21,099
I don't mind flying, but what you're doing is suicide!

415
00:32:21,099 --> 00:32:27,139
Let's take Luke Skywalker's Landspeeder, for example.

416
00:32:27,140 --> 00:32:35,140
George Lucas described it as an auto-like transport that travels a few feet above the ground on a magnetic field.

417
00:32:35,140 --> 00:32:46,140
Magnetic levitation, or maglev technology, actually exists and is based on the fact that two-like magnetic poles repel one another.

418
00:32:46,140 --> 00:32:52,140
Maglev trains use this principle and operate by moving a magnet attached to the train.

419
00:32:52,140 --> 00:32:59,140
The action generates a magnetic field, which lifts and propels the vehicle down the tracks on a cushion of air.

420
00:32:59,140 --> 00:33:07,140
But how would maglev technology work on Luke's home planet, Tatooine, where there don't appear to be any tracks?

421
00:33:07,140 --> 00:33:11,140
You could do that if the planet's made out of the right magnetic materials.

422
00:33:11,140 --> 00:33:20,140
Something like iron or nickel that have a strong magnetic response, your ship could generate a magnetic field that would then cause you to lift above that material.

423
00:33:20,140 --> 00:33:25,140
But what if a planet didn't have the apparent magnetic properties of Tatooine?

424
00:33:25,140 --> 00:33:28,140
A place like Coruscant, for instance.

425
00:33:28,140 --> 00:33:32,140
How did the air speeders of that world fly through the sky without wings?

426
00:33:32,140 --> 00:33:37,140
Since these things seem to zip around just about any place and at any altitude you want,

427
00:33:37,140 --> 00:33:43,140
it would require something that we don't have yet, pretty much anti-gravity.

428
00:33:43,140 --> 00:33:45,140
We know about magnetism.

429
00:33:45,140 --> 00:33:48,140
Charged particles attract and repel.

430
00:33:48,140 --> 00:33:51,140
So one might think I could get gravity to repel.

431
00:33:51,140 --> 00:33:55,140
It turns out within our universe we know that you can't make gravity repulsive.

432
00:33:55,140 --> 00:34:02,140
However, you can look at theories of physics that are consistent within themselves and could contain other forces that would act like anti-gravity.

433
00:34:04,140 --> 00:34:10,140
You can imagine in a completely different universe, you might get a force that can be repulsive relative to gravity.

434
00:34:10,139 --> 00:34:15,139
And then you can imagine using that as a technology to generate these things that float.

435
00:34:15,139 --> 00:34:20,139
In the Star Wars universe, many vehicles use this notion of anti-gravity.

436
00:34:20,139 --> 00:34:30,139
George Lucas called it repulsor lift technology, which allows crafts of every kind to lift off the ground, hover, and even sail through the sky.

437
00:34:30,139 --> 00:34:35,139
It seems like in the Star Wars universe they have the power to manipulate gravity.

438
00:34:35,139 --> 00:34:42,139
If they can control gravity, they can make repulsor lift vehicles of any kind.

439
00:34:42,139 --> 00:34:47,139
Using repulsor lift technology to fly above a planet's surface is one thing.

440
00:34:47,139 --> 00:34:55,139
But just how do Luke, Leia, and those nasty stormtroopers zip through giant redwoods on the moon of Endor without ever hitting a tree?

441
00:34:57,139 --> 00:34:59,139
Well, almost never.

442
00:35:00,139 --> 00:35:07,139
According to Star Wars experts, the answer to why stormtroopers are so quick can be found right under their high-tech helmets.

443
00:35:07,139 --> 00:35:12,139
I was reading an article recently about guys who race on motorcycles.

444
00:35:15,139 --> 00:35:28,139
